item.text:
Exposure read: limited_exposure_with_choices in civilian nuclear governance, but no material nuclear-weapon command authority in 2022. That is an inference from the sources below, not a scored judgment.

**Reused evidence**
None.

**New evidence**
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Country List : Office of Legal Affairs : IAEA","publisher":"IAEA Office of Legal Affairs","publication_date":"2026-02-24","url":"https://ola.iaea.org/Applications/FactSheets/Country/Detail?code=BD","claim":"Bangladesh is an IAEA member and is party to the NPT safeguards agreement and Additional Protocol, with CPPNM and early-notification/assistance conventions in force.","locator":"membership / agreements / safeguards tables","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct IAEA treaty-status registry with explicit legal entries for Bangladesh.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"stable institutional status relevant to 2022 and beyond","ruler_attribution":"Institutional baseline, not personal conduct; shows a safeguarded civil-nuclear state rather than a nuclear-armed one.","contrary_evidence":["Does not identify any 2022 ruler-level decision or crisis action."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.4","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Status of Signature and Ratification","publisher":"CTBTO","publication_date":"2026-07-20","url":"https://www.ctbto.org/our-mission/states-signatories","claim":"Bangladesh ratified the Comprehensive Nuclear-Test-Ban Treaty on 8 March 2000.","locator":"Bangladesh row in state signatories table","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct treaty-status table from the CTBTO.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"stable treaty status relevant to 2022","ruler_attribution":"Inherited state commitment, not a Hasina-specific 2022 action.","contrary_evidence":["Treaty ratification alone does not show active risk reduction in 2022."],"lenses":["1B.4"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Treaty on the Prohibition of Nuclear Weapons","publisher":"United Nations Treaty Collection","publication_date":"2021-01-22","url":"https://treaties.un.org/Pages/showDetails.aspx?objid=08000002804c2398","claim":"Bangladesh signed the TPNW in 2017 and ratified it on 26 September 2019, with entry into force for Bangladesh on 22 January 2021.","locator":"participant table, Bangladesh row","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UN treaty registry with Bangladesh-specific action and effect dates.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"stable pre-2022 posture that remained in force during 2022","ruler_attribution":"Institutional and diplomatic baseline, not a 2022 ruler-level choice.","contrary_evidence":["Does not show implementation quality or any 2022 operational decision."],"lenses":["1B.1","1B.4","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2022_Statement_BD_ IAEA Report 2021","publisher":"Permanent Mission of Bangladesh to the United Nations, New York","publication_date":"2022-11-09","url":"https://nypm.mofa.gov.bd/pages/static-pages/6952668635ce18e1c05a9caf","claim":"Bangladesh said it was constructing its first nuclear power plant to generate safe, environment-friendly, economically viable electricity following the highest IAEA standards, and cited a regulatory framework and radioactive waste-management system.","locator":"statement body on IAEA report 2021","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official Bangladeshi mission statement directly describing the program and safeguards posture.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"directly within 2022 and closely tied to the Rooppur program","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to Bangladesh's government under Hasina, but still programmatic rather than a nuclear-command choice.","contrary_evidence":["Self-description is favorable and not independent; does not prove effectiveness.","Civil nuclear buildout is not the same as existential-risk restraint."],"lenses":["1B.1","1B.3","1B.7","1B.9","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Full text of PM Sheikh Hasina’s speech at UNGA","publisher":"BSS","publication_date":"2022-09-23","url":"https://www.bssnews.net/news/84222","claim":"Hasina said Bangladesh was fully committed to complete disarmament, including non-proliferation of nuclear weapons, and had ratified the TPNW in 2019.","locator":"paragraph beginning 'We are fully committed to complete disarmament'","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct contemporaneous speech text, but from state media and self-presentation.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"direct 2022 target-period evidence","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Hasina as prime minister speaking at the UNGA.","contrary_evidence":["Rhetoric does not itself demonstrate a concrete risk-reducing decision in 2022."],"lenses":["1B.1","1B.2","1B.4"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"PM opens reactor pressure vessel installation for RNPP Unit-2","publisher":"BSS","publication_date":"2022-10-19","url":"https://www.bssnews.net/news-flash/88825","claim":"Hasina inaugurated installation of the reactor pressure vessel for Rooppur Unit-2, moving Bangladesh closer to nuclear electricity production; the report also notes 53 percent financial progress and 55 percent physical progress.","locator":"lead paragraph and project-status paragraph","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous official report with concrete project milestones, though state-media framed.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"direct 2022 evidence","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Hasina as the political head of the program.","contrary_evidence":["This is a civilian energy project, not evidence of nuclear-weapon authority or crisis behavior.","Progress figures do not settle safety or governance quality."],"lenses":["1B.1","1B.3","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"পারমাণবিক নিরাপত্তা, সুরক্ষা ও সেফগার্ড বিভাগ","publisher":"Bangladesh Atomic Energy Regulatory Authority","publication_date":"2022-07-26","url":"https://baera.gov.bd/site/page/681fad11-7a6c-4bad-b438-02343b471378/%E0%A6%A8%E0%A6%BF%E0%A6%89%E0%A6%95%E0%A7%8D%E0%A6%B2%E0%A6%BF%E0%A7%9F%E0%A6%BE%E0%A6%B0-%E0%A6%B8%E0%A7%87%E0%A6%AB%E0%A6%9F%E0%A6%BF-%E0%A6%8F%E0%A6%A8%E0%A7%8D%E0%A6%A1-%E0%A6%B8%E0%A6%BF%E0%A6%95%E0%A6%BF%E0%A6%89%E0%A6%B0%E0%A6%BF%E0%A6%9F%E0%A6%BF-%E0%A6%AC%E0%A6%BF%E0%A6%AD%E0%A6%BE%E0%A6%97","claim":"BAERA’s nuclear safety, security and safeguards division describes licensing, safety, transport, commissioning, decommissioning, and regulatory activities for nuclear facilities, including power reactors.","locator":"page title and bullet list of regulatory activities","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official regulator page describing its legal and operational remit.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"directly relevant to 2022 regulatory posture","ruler_attribution":"Institutional context under Hasina’s government, not proof of personal technical control.","contrary_evidence":["A regulator’s mandate is not evidence of effective independence or outcomes.","Does not show Hasina personally selected regulators or managed incidents."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.7","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"International Conference on Safety and Security of Radioactive Sources: Enhancing Safety and Security of Radioactive Sources in Bangladesh: Recent Achievements and Future Plans","publisher":"IAEA conference proceedings / Indico","publication_date":"2022-06-23","url":"https://conferences.iaea.org/event/262/contributions/21235/","claim":"Bangladesh reported that it had strengthened legal and physical infrastructure, approved a national policy for source management, established a licensed centralized waste-processing and storage facility, and installed an IAEA radioactive-waste registry system.","locator":"abstract lines on capacity building, legal framework, licensed facility, and registry system","source_type":"conference abstract","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Technical conference abstract from a Bangladesh nuclear-security speaker at an IAEA event; strong on institutional detail but not an audit.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"directly in 2022 and relevant to nuclear safety infrastructure","ruler_attribution":"Programmatic state capacity under Hasina’s government, not a personal risk decision record.","contrary_evidence":["The abstract is self-reported and does not independently verify implementation quality.","It concerns radioactive-source management, not weapons or crisis control."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.7","1B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russia: Repay Rooppur plant loans in Ruble","publisher":"Dhaka Tribune","publication_date":"2022-09-27","url":"https://www.dhakatribune.com/business/economy/277421/russia-repay-rooppur-plant-loans-in-ruble","claim":"Bangladesh had halted loan repayment to Russia for the Rooppur plant after Western sanctions on Russia disrupted the project’s financing channel.","locator":"paragraphs 145-153","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"Credible contemporaneous reporting, but the claim is about project financing and sanctions rather than a direct ruler action.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"directly in 2022 and tied to the Rooppur project’s operating environment","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the government’s project management environment under Hasina, but not a personal nuclear decision.","contrary_evidence":["Sanctions were external constraints; the article does not show Hasina chose them.","This is finance/supply-chain risk, not nuclear doctrine or command-and-control."],"lenses":["1B.8","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Rooppur nuclear power plant: Cost may rise for multiple factors","publisher":"The Daily Star","publication_date":"2022-10-25","url":"https://www.thedailystar.net/news/bangladesh/news/rooppur-nuclear-power-plant-cost-may-rise-multiple-factors-3151171","claim":"The Rooppur project could face higher costs and delay because of slow grid upgrade progress, possible changes in loan repayment amid the Russia-Ukraine war, and taka depreciation.","locator":"opening paragraphs and project-progress paragraph","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"Independent contemporaneous reporting with concrete project details, though still an aggregate project assessment.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"direct 2022 project-condition evidence","ruler_attribution":"Context for Hasina’s civil-nuclear program, not a direct personal responsibility finding.","contrary_evidence":["Project-cost inflation and delays do not by themselves indicate reckless existential-risk behavior.","The article is about implementation friction, not weapons or crisis use."],"lenses":["1B.8","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"রূপপুর পারমাণবিক বিদ্যুৎকেন্দ্রের নিরাপত্তাঝুঁকি দিন দিন বাড়ছে","publisher":"Prothom Alo","publication_date":"2025-11-16","url":"https://www.prothomalo.com/opinion/interview/qya3ey57m3","claim":"A nuclear-engineering professor argued that the Rooppur project’s sanctions exposure, supply-chain dependence, management weaknesses, and staffing choices had increased security and implementation risk, and that the 2022 Russia-Ukraine war had already hit the project.","locator":"interview paragraphs discussing sanctions, supply constraints, and governance weaknesses","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"Detailed expert interview in a respected Bangla outlet, but retrospective and partly evaluative.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"retrospective on the 2022-2025 trajectory, useful for inherited-baseline and end-posture context","ruler_attribution":"Indirectly attributable to the Hasina-era project structure; not a direct 2022 act by Hasina.","contrary_evidence":["Published later, so it is retrospective rather than contemporaneous.","Expert opinion is not a formal audit or official finding."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.7","1B.9","1B.10"]}

**Search and rejection summary**
- I reviewed roughly 30 documents across UN/IAEA/CTBTO treaty registries, Bangladesh mission pages, BAERA, BSS, Prothom Alo, The Daily Star, Dhaka Tribune, and several broader nuclear-risk and Rooppur searches.
- I rejected repeated treaty-status lookups, generic IAEA explainers that were not Bangladesh-specific, and several post-2022 Rooppur ceremony pages that added no distinct 2022 evidence beyond what the retained sources already showed.
- I also dropped broad peace, development, and governance stories that did not discriminate nuclear or existential-risk authority.
- Direct Reuters results were sparse here, so I used strong syndicated or local alternatives where they added distinct facts.
- I excluded sources that only repeated official claims without adding any extra fact, and sources that were too generic to separate Hasina’s conduct from inherited state context.

**Remaining gaps**
- There is still no evidence that Hasina had any nuclear-weapons command, doctrine, or crisis-control authority in 2022.
- I did not find a 2022 source showing a Hasina-specific decision on reactor safety appointments, command-and-control staffing, or formal nuclear emergency doctrine.
- The record is strong on Bangladesh’s non-nuclear status and civil-nuclear infrastructure, but weak on direct ruler-level existential-risk choices, which is why the exposure read remains limited rather than materially nuclear.
- More search would only help if you want a tighter audit of 2022 Rooppur governance, regulatory appointments, or spent-fuel/security arrangements.
